# PARITY fixture, converted FROM a refusal — the best ending a guard can have.
#
# This file spent one round in refuse/: the VM loop-scopes a function-body
# binder over a module-bound name, and the guard refused what one flat C
# variable could not express. The next review showed the refusal EVICTED DMG
# from the envelope (`i is 1` at module scope vs `for i in range of 23040` in
# a render function — the census dropped 87→86 and the flip was the flagship).
# Loop-scoping was emittable all along: DECLARING the same C name inside the
# loop body makes C block scoping reproduce the VM's chain walk, the #109
# numeric-shadow move. The rows below were silently wrong at main, refused for
# one round, and are now simply ANSWERED.
#
#
# A function-body `for` binder over a PLAIN module global — no parameter, no
# local declaration, just the module's own name used as a binder inside a
# function.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is that the guard's first version keyed on
# parameter-ness, and the rule is about module-bound-ness. Measured:
#
#   x is 5
#   define f(a) as: for x in [7, 8]: local q is x / return x
#   VM   f of 1 -> 5, module x stays 5     (binder loop-scoped)
#   AOT  f of 1 -> 8, and x became 8       (silent, both rc 0, PRE-EXISTING)
#
# At MODULE scope the same binder persists (measured in an earlier round: the
# VM prints 3 after `for x in [1,2,3]`, not the incoming value) — so the rule
# is "function body + module-bound name", and the three shapes (param shadow,
# local shadow, plain global) are one refusal, not three.

x is 5

define f(a) as:
    for x in [7, 8]:
        local q is x
    return x

print of (f of 1)
print of x
